# web_tool — an impure-boundary PORT for web fetch / search, declared in the
# verb/produces (effect-with-verdict) form, mirroring llm.port.
#
# Converted from the dead adapters/web_tool/web_tool.family (parser-removal
# plan, Phase 1a — `.family` was never a parsed hecksagain construct). Lives
# at the repo-root ports/ directory — see messaging.port's own comment for why.
#
# NOTE: hecks_conception/aggregates/framework/adapter_families/web_tool.hecksagon
# is a DISTINCT, OLDER construct (`Hecks.adapter_family` — io-adapter form :
# trigger_field/behaviors, NO verb/produces — the pre-hecksagain in-process
# web_tool_dispatcher.rs fallback). Checked, not assumed : `Hecks.adapter_family`
# does not exist anywhere in hecksagain (grepped), and `hecksagain-cli validate
# hecks_conception` passes clean with that file present — so it is not actually
# being loaded/executed by the corpus's real boot path today, dead content, not a
# live collision risk. This port (`fetched_by`, real, loaded, live) is unrelated.
#
# Signal is EFFECT, WITH A VERDICT (like llm, unlike tts) : the aggregate EMITS an
# event carrying the url / query, the adapter performs the web call
# OUT-OF-PROCESS, and the result RE-ENTERS the domain as a verdict command
# (success "Agg.RecordResult"). The port declares `produces :output`, and the
# bind carries a `do success/failure end` block whose success verdict receives the
# `output` the handler emits.
#
# The how-verb is `fetched_by`, hung off the aggregate FQN with its triggering
# event (`on:`). Per-deployment config (tool, user_agent, fixtures) lives on the
# adapter that implements this port and in .world.
Hecks.port "web_tool" do
  verb     "fetched_by"
  signal   :effect
  produces :output
end
